00:42:05,324 --> 00:42:06,604
lead with:

766
00:42:06,604 --> 00:42:11,464
without a lender of last resort, banks fail, depositors lose their savings,

767
00:42:11,524 --> 00:42:13,004
and the economy collapses.

768
00:42:13,464 --> 00:42:17,324
The Federal Reserve was created in nineteen thirteen specifically because

769
00:42:17,324 --> 00:42:21,324
the Panic of nineteen oh-seven frightened everyone into agreeing that a

770
00:42:21,324 --> 00:42:22,844
central bank was necessary.

771
00:42:23,364 --> 00:42:27,484
The Fed then spent the nineteen twenties expanding credit, inflating the

772
00:42:27,484 --> 00:42:30,184
bubble whose collapse it was created to prevent.

773
00:42:31,064 --> 00:42:34,814
Sixteen years later, more than nine thousand banks failed.

774
00:42:35,064 --> 00:42:39,324
The institution created specifically to prevent banking panics contributed

775
00:42:39,324 --> 00:42:42,184
to the worst banking panic in your history.

776
00:42:42,904 --> 00:42:45,784
Canada did not have a central bank until nineteen thirty-five.

777
00:42:46,444 --> 00:42:51,364
During the same Depression, same continent, same decade, Canada had zero

778
00:42:51,364 --> 00:42:52,424
bank failures.

779
00:42:52,924 --> 00:42:57,044
The country next door, without the institution you said was necessary, had

780
00:42:57,044 --> 00:42:59,264
the outcome you said was impossible.

781
00:42:59,784 --> 00:43:02,544
The country with the central bank had the crisis.

782
00:43:02,984 --> 00:43:04,804
The country without one didn't.

783
00:43:05,464 --> 00:43:07,524
Your economists treat this as a coincidence.

784
00:43:08,524 --> 00:43:12,264
What actually stopped bank runs was not the Federal Reserve.

785
00:43:12,664 --> 00:43:16,944
It was the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, created in nineteen thirty-three,

786
00:43:17,664 --> 00:43:18,964
which guaranteed deposits.

787
00:43:19,384 --> 00:43:21,374
A bank run is a coordination problem:

788
00:43:22,164 --> 00:43:26,164
everyone withdraws because they fear everyone else will withdraw first.

789
00:43:26,884 --> 00:43:27,844
Insurance solves it.

790
00:43:28,404 --> 00:43:31,954
You do not need a money printer to solve a coordination problem.

791
00:43:32,364 --> 00:43:35,284
You need a promise, a small premium, and a ledger.

792
00:43:35,764 --> 00:43:38,824
You solved bank runs in nineteen thirty-three with insurance.

793
00:43:39,544 --> 00:43:41,464
You kept the institution that caused them.

794
00:43:42,144 --> 00:43:45,974
On Wishonia, when we discover the medicine is making the patient sicker,

795
00:43:46,024 --> 00:43:47,364
we stop administering it.

796
00:43:47,884 --> 00:43:51,504
On Earth, you doubled the dose and gave the pharmacist a raise.

797
00:43:52,624 --> 00:43:53,844
What They Could Measure Instead.

798
00:43:54,984 --> 00:43:58,224
Here is a question that is apparently too obvious for economics:

799
00:43:59,004 --> 00:44:01,404
Are people's lives actually getting better?

800
00:44:01,984 --> 00:44:02,484
That's it.

801
00:44:02,884 --> 00:44:03,864
You could measure:

802
00:44:03,944 --> 00:44:06,884
Inflation-adjusted after-tax median income:

803
00:44:07,384 --> 00:44:12,414
Are people actually richer, or just holding bigger numbers on smaller paychecks?

804
00:44:12,764 --> 00:44:14,884
Median health-adjusted life years:

805
00:44:15,544 --> 00:44:19,924
Are people living longer, healthier lives, or just working longer before

806
00:44:19,924 --> 00:44:23,924
dying of diseases you could have cured with the money you printed for banks?

807
00:44:24,704 --> 00:44:26,074
These metrics exist.

808
00:44:26,464 --> 00:44:28,784
Economists know how to calculate them.

809
00:44:28,984 --> 00:44:31,084
No central bank on Earth uses them.

810
00:44:31,624 --> 00:44:35,644
Because if the Federal Reserve were graded on whether the median American

811
00:44:35,644 --> 00:44:39,964
is healthier and wealthier each year, someone would have to explain why

812
00:44:39,964 --> 00:44:43,384
the answer has been "no" for most of the past five decades.

813
00:44:44,024 --> 00:44:48,664
It is much easier to aim for "maximum employment" and "two percent inflation"

814
00:44:48,924 --> 00:44:52,884
and declare success while everyone works more hours for less purchasing

815
00:44:52,884 --> 00:44:53,384
power.

816
00:44:54,644 --> 00:44:55,964
How the Robbery Works.

817
00:44:56,744 --> 00:45:00,924
In the seventeen hundreds, a human named Richard Cantillon noticed something

818
00:45:00,924 --> 00:45:04,064
that everyone already knew but nobody had written down:

819
00:45:04,664 --> 00:45:08,864
when you create new money, whoever gets it first benefits, and whoever gets

820
00:45:08,864 --> 00:45:09,964
it last pays.

821
00:45:10,644 --> 00:45:13,324
On Wishonia, this is called "how money works."

822
00:45:13,724 --> 00:45:17,704
On Earth, it is taught as a niche economic theory, if it is taught at all.

823
00:45:18,544 --> 00:45:19,864
Here's what it looks like:

824
00:45:19,904 --> 00:45:21,344
The Fed creates new money.

825
00:45:21,844 --> 00:45:24,304
Banks and government contractors receive it first.

826
00:45:24,824 --> 00:45:26,664
They buy things at today's prices.

827
00:45:26,984 --> 00:45:27,764
Prices rise.

828
00:45:28,164 --> 00:45:32,244
By the time the new money reaches your grocery store, everything costs more.

829
00:45:32,704 --> 00:45:36,524
You buy the same groceries for more money and conclude that groceries have

830
00:45:36,524 --> 00:45:37,524
gotten expensive.

831
00:45:38,104 --> 00:45:39,754
Groceries did not get expensive.

832
00:45:40,204 --> 00:45:40,964
Your money got cheaper.

833
00:45:41,864 --> 00:45:45,924
But "your money got cheaper" requires a culprit, and culprits are boring

834
00:45:45,924 --> 00:45:48,244
when they wear suits and speak in percentages.

835
00:45:48,704 --> 00:45:51,364
So instead you get angry about the price of eggs.

836
00:45:52,044 --> 00:45:54,044
On Wishonia, we have a saying:

837
00:45:54,044 --> 00:45:58,024
"If your eggs cost more every year but your chickens didn't change, look

838
00:45:58,024 --> 00:45:59,744
for whoever is shrinking your money."

839
00:46:00,304 --> 00:46:02,184
We don't actually have this saying.

840
00:46:02,204 --> 00:46:02,764
But we should.

841
00:46:03,964 --> 00:46:05,364
The Spread.

842
00:46:05,364 --> 00:46:08,824
There is an even simpler version of this trick, and you can watch it happen

843
00:46:08,924 --> 00:46:12,564
to you personally, right now, on your phone, if you check your credit card

844
00:46:12,564 --> 00:46:13,064
statement.

845
00:46:13,584 --> 00:46:14,084
I'll wait.

846
00:46:14,664 --> 00:46:18,124
The Federal Reserve lends money to banks at about five percent.

847
00:46:18,664 --> 00:46:19,264
Sometimes less.

848
00:46:19,704 --> 00:46:23,484
After two thousand and eight, it lent money to banks at nearly zero percent

849
00:46:23,484 --> 00:46:27,704
for fourteen years, which is a very generous interest rate, in the same

850
00:46:27,704 --> 00:46:30,204
way that "free" is a very generous price.

851
00:46:30,944 --> 00:46:33,964
Banks then lend that money to you at twenty-four percent.

852
00:46:34,544 --> 00:46:36,504
I want to make sure you saw both numbers.

853
00:46:36,944 --> 00:46:39,304
The bank borrows money for zero percent.

854
00:46:39,784 --> 00:46:43,124
The bank lends you that same money for twenty-four percent.

855
00:46:43,884 --> 00:46:45,414
The money has not changed.

856
00:46:45,664 --> 00:46:48,584
It did not improve during its brief stay at the bank.

857
00:46:49,244 --> 00:46:51,444
Nobody at the bank added anything to it.

858
00:46:51,824 --> 00:46:55,964
It just sat in the bank for a bit and came out twenty-four percent more

859
00:46:55,964 --> 00:46:58,704
expensive, like a bottle of wine at a restaurant,

860
00:46:59,504 --> 00:47:03,604
except the wine is imaginary and the restaurant prints its own wine.

861
00:47:04,384 --> 00:47:06,504
You cannot borrow from the Fed yourself.

862
00:47:07,004 --> 00:47:07,504
I checked.

863
00:47:07,924 --> 00:47:11,804
There is a list of who is allowed to borrow from the Fed, and the list is:

864
00:47:12,004 --> 00:47:12,664
banks.

865
00:47:12,784 --> 00:47:13,804
Not you.

866
00:47:13,804 --> 00:47:14,644
Not your business.

867
00:47:15,124 --> 00:47:17,224
Not your mother, even if she asks nicely.

868
00:47:18,004 --> 00:47:18,504
Banks.

869
00:47:19,224 --> 00:47:23,004
So you borrow from the bank, who borrowed from the Fed, who made the money

870
00:47:23,004 --> 00:47:23,724
out of nothing.

871
00:47:24,624 --> 00:47:29,044
You are paying twenty-four percent annual interest on money that was typed

872
00:47:29,044 --> 00:47:33,804
into a computer for free by a person you will never meet, in a building

873
00:47:33,804 --> 00:47:38,044
you are not allowed to enter, and the twenty-four percent goes to the people

874
00:47:38,044 --> 00:47:39,144
who own the building.

875
00:47:39,824 --> 00:47:44,304
Every single point of interest is a fee for not being in the room when the

876
00:47:44,304 --> 00:47:45,284
money was invented.

877
00:47:46,384 --> 00:47:49,044
On Wishonia, we had a similar arrangement once.

878
00:47:49,884 --> 00:47:53,504
Someone would stand between the river and the village and charge people

879
00:47:53,504 --> 00:47:54,444
to carry water.

880
00:47:55,064 --> 00:47:58,304
We did not call this person a "financial intermediary."

881
00:47:58,744 --> 00:48:02,284
We called them a "water thief" and moved them away from the river.

882
00:48:02,844 --> 00:48:03,704
It took about a day.

883
00:48:04,644 --> 00:48:06,784
Your version has been running for a century.

884
00:48:07,244 --> 00:48:08,824
The water thief has a logo.

885
00:48:09,464 --> 00:48:10,664
The twenty twenty Demonstration.

886
00:48:11,984 --> 00:48:16,684
In twenty twenty, the Fed created roughly four trillion dollars in new money.

887
00:48:17,424 --> 00:48:20,664
I need to give you a unit of measurement for that number, because large

888
00:48:20,664 --> 00:48:23,914
numbers are where your species hides things.

889
00:48:24,324 --> 00:48:28,924
Your governments spend four point five billion dollars per year on clinical

890
00:48:28,924 --> 00:48:29,484
trials.

891
00:48:30,084 --> 00:48:34,164
That is the part of medicine where you actually test whether drugs work

892
00:48:34,164 --> 00:48:34,864
in humans.

893
00:48:35,504 --> 00:48:39,944
It is the entire global budget for finding out which chemicals stop you

894
00:48:39,944 --> 00:48:40,584
from dying.

895
00:48:41,184 --> 00:48:45,164
Every government on Earth, combined, pooling everything they have:

896
00:48:46,004 --> 00:48:48,264
four point five billion dollars.

897
00:48:49,064 --> 00:48:49,864
Where did it go?

898
00:48:50,604 --> 00:48:54,444
This is a mystery in the same way that asking "who ate the cake?"

899
00:48:54,864 --> 00:48:58,604
is a mystery when there is one person in the room covered in frosting.

900
00:48:59,364 --> 00:49:04,864
The Top one percent gained exactly four trillion dollars in net worth over

901
00:49:04,864 --> 00:49:05,824
the same period.

902
00:49:06,344 --> 00:49:08,484
The Fed printed four trillion dollars.

903
00:49:08,844 --> 00:49:11,144
The rich gained four trillion dollars.

904
00:49:11,584 --> 00:49:14,324
On Wishonia, we would call this a "clue."

905
00:49:14,984 --> 00:49:17,664
On Earth, you called it "a robust recovery.".

906
00:49:18,864 --> 00:49:23,184
Nearly ninety percent of those gains came from stocks, which is what the

907
00:49:23,304 --> 00:49:25,064
Fed's new money inflated first.

908
00:49:25,744 --> 00:49:30,424
Only one-third of bottom-fifty percent households own any stocks at all.

909
00:49:31,024 --> 00:49:35,884
The Fed inflated the one asset that poor people don't have, then issued

910
00:49:35,884 --> 00:49:39,204
a report expressing concern about inequality.

911
00:49:39,704 --> 00:49:44,064
On Wishonia, this is called "setting the building on fire and then volunteering

912
00:49:44,064 --> 00:49:45,004
for the fire department.".

913
00:49:46,324 --> 00:49:47,684
The Fed printed the money.

914
00:49:48,024 --> 00:49:49,644
The stock market absorbed it.

915
00:49:50,064 --> 00:49:51,624
The rich owned the stocks.

916
00:49:52,144 --> 00:49:53,784
The poor got the price increases.

917
00:49:54,644 --> 00:49:56,884
Cantillon, writing in the seventeen hundreds,

918
00:49:57,644 --> 00:49:59,284
predicted this mechanism exactly.

919
00:50:00,084 --> 00:50:01,264
He would not be surprised.

920
00:50:01,744 --> 00:50:03,844
He would be annoyed that you are still surprised.

921
00:50:05,104 --> 00:50:06,004
The Theft That Followed.

922
00:50:07,424 --> 00:50:09,004
Since nineteen seventy-two:

923
00:50:09,784 --> 00:50:13,064
Productivity went up two hundred and forty-six percent.

924
00:50:13,824 --> 00:50:16,504
Wages went up one hundred and fifteen percent.

925
00:50:17,204 --> 00:50:19,844
I stared at these two numbers for a long time.

926
00:50:20,524 --> 00:50:21,684
Then I checked them.

927
00:50:22,204 --> 00:50:25,884
Then I checked them again, because I assumed I was reading the wrong chart,

928
00:50:26,164 --> 00:50:27,244
or the wrong planet.

929
00:50:28,184 --> 00:50:28,824
I was not.

930
00:50:29,844 --> 00:50:34,884
Your species did two hundred and forty-six percent more work and received

931
00:50:34,884 --> 00:50:37,644
one hundred and fifteen percent more pay.

932
00:50:38,344 --> 00:50:41,424
The remaining one hundred and thirty-one percent went somewhere.

933
00:50:42,184 --> 00:50:44,124
It was not to the people who did the work.

934
00:50:45,204 --> 00:50:47,464
On Wishonia, this is called "theft."

935
00:50:48,344 --> 00:50:50,524
On Earth, it is called "the economy."

936
00:50:51,364 --> 00:50:52,604
These are the same word.

937
00:50:53,124 --> 00:50:54,804
Yours just has more syllables.

938
00:50:56,164 --> 00:50:57,484
The Two-Income Trap.

939
00:50:58,804 --> 00:51:03,804
In nineteen sixty-seven, fewer than half of married-couple families had

940
00:51:03,804 --> 00:51:05,004
both spouses working.

941
00:51:06,004 --> 00:51:10,604
One paycheck bought a house, a car, annual vacations, and a pension.

942
00:51:11,384 --> 00:51:16,644
The other adult raised children, or gardened, or was bored.

943
00:51:16,744 --> 00:51:19,904
Boredom was a luxury your grandparents could afford.

944
00:51:20,544 --> 00:51:24,684
They could afford it because one human working forty hours a week earned

945
00:51:24,684 --> 00:51:29,324
enough to house, feed, transport, and retire an entire family.

946
00:51:29,904 --> 00:51:32,034
I want you to read that sentence again,

947
00:51:32,944 --> 00:51:33,444
slowly,

948
00:51:33,724 --> 00:51:36,004
because I can feel you not believing it.

949
00:51:37,524 --> 00:51:38,574
By twenty eleven,

950
00:51:39,604 --> 00:51:43,624
husband-only earner families had dropped from thirty-six percent to nineteen

951
00:51:43,624 --> 00:51:44,124
percent.

952
00:51:45,024 --> 00:51:49,504
The rest sent both adults to work and paid strangers to raise their children.

953
00:51:50,624 --> 00:51:54,564
Working wives contributed twenty-seven percent of family income in nineteen

954
00:51:54,564 --> 00:51:55,414
seventy.

955
00:51:55,944 --> 00:51:58,804
By twenty eleven, it was thirty-seven percent.

956
00:51:59,704 --> 00:52:05,164
An entire additional human entered the workforce and the family gained ten

957
00:52:05,164 --> 00:52:05,904
percentage points.

958
00:52:07,344 --> 00:52:10,024
I want to be very precise about what happened here.

959
00:52:10,944 --> 00:52:14,604
Your species noticed that one paycheck was no longer sufficient,

960
00:52:15,204 --> 00:52:17,604
so it doubled the number of workers per household.

961
00:52:18,544 --> 00:52:23,304
The two paychecks combined now purchase a lifestyle that one paycheck covered

962
00:52:23,304 --> 00:52:25,204
comfortably in nineteen seventy-one.

963
00:52:25,944 --> 00:52:30,404
You added a whole extra human to the economy and broke even.

964
00:52:31,364 --> 00:52:35,884
On Wishonia, when you double the inputs and the outputs stay the same, you

965
00:52:35,884 --> 00:52:36,764
check for parasites.

966
00:52:38,004 --> 00:52:41,284
On Earth, you invented the phrase "work-life balance,"

967
00:52:42,004 --> 00:52:46,504
which describes having half of what your parents had but in two directions

968
00:52:46,504 --> 00:52:47,604
simultaneously.

969
00:52:49,224 --> 00:52:50,564
The Time They Broke It.

970
00:52:51,884 --> 00:52:54,424
Remember the spread from a few paragraphs ago.

971
00:52:55,004 --> 00:52:57,004
The Fed creates money from nothing.

972
00:52:57,424 --> 00:52:59,544
It lends this invented money to banks.

973
00:52:59,964 --> 00:53:04,924
The banks' one job, the entire reason society tolerates their existence,

974
00:53:05,384 --> 00:53:08,184
is to move that money from savers to borrowers.

975
00:53:08,964 --> 00:53:09,464
A ledger.

976
00:53:10,024 --> 00:53:10,524
Arithmetic.

977
00:53:11,304 --> 00:53:15,904
A task so simple that an eleven-year-old on Wishonia automated it for a

978
00:53:15,904 --> 00:53:16,584
science fair.

979
00:53:17,104 --> 00:53:21,424
In two thousand and eight, they took that money (money that cost them nothing,

980
00:53:21,724 --> 00:53:25,824
that they did not earn, that was conjured from a spreadsheet by a committee

981
00:53:25,824 --> 00:53:30,184
they play golf with) and built a casino on top of people's houses.

982
00:53:31,144 --> 00:53:34,584
They made bets on whether homeowners would keep paying their mortgages,

983
00:53:35,164 --> 00:53:39,624
then made bets on those bets, then made bets on the bets on the bets.

984
00:53:40,084 --> 00:53:44,104
I tried to explain this to someone on Wishonia and they asked me to start

985
00:53:44,104 --> 00:53:47,514
over because they assumed I was having a malfunction.

986
00:53:48,264 --> 00:53:49,534
I was not having a malfunction.

987
00:53:50,364 --> 00:53:56,024
Your banks really did bet on bets on bets, the way a child might dare another

988
00:53:56,024 --> 00:53:58,744
child to dare a third child to eat dirt.

989
00:53:59,844 --> 00:54:04,204
Except the dirt was people's houses, and the children were wearing four

990
00:54:04,204 --> 00:54:08,864
thousand dollar suits purchased with the interest spread on money that was

991
00:54:08,864 --> 00:54:10,364
imaginary before breakfast.

992
00:54:11,404 --> 00:54:13,144
None of this was their job.

993
00:54:13,484 --> 00:54:15,824
Their job was maintaining the spreadsheet.

994
00:54:16,404 --> 00:54:18,054
They were not hired to gamble.

995
00:54:18,384 --> 00:54:19,814
They were hired to do arithmetic.

996
00:54:20,564 --> 00:54:24,824
They got bored of arithmetic, because arithmetic doesn't pay bonuses large

997
00:54:24,824 --> 00:54:29,004
enough to buy a yacht, and they discovered that gambling with free money

998
00:54:29,004 --> 00:54:33,504
is extraordinarily profitable right up until it isn't.

999
00:54:34,284 --> 00:54:35,724
The houses were real.

1000
00:54:36,184 --> 00:54:37,284
The bets were not.

1001
00:54:37,904 --> 00:54:38,784
When the bets collapsed,

1002
00:54:39,524 --> 00:54:42,004
ten million Americans lost their homes.

1003
00:54:42,564 --> 00:54:46,784
Then your banks did something I still find remarkable, even after eighty

1004
00:54:46,784 --> 00:54:48,604
years of watching your species.

1005
00:54:49,184 --> 00:54:50,624
They called it a "crisis."

1006
00:54:51,164 --> 00:54:55,624
I want to examine this word, because it is doing more work than any word

1007
00:54:55,624 --> 00:54:56,444
has ever done.

1008
00:54:57,164 --> 00:54:58,944
The money was created from nothing.

1009
00:54:59,344 --> 00:55:00,864
The buildings were still standing.

1010
00:55:01,224 --> 00:55:02,824
The factories still existed.

1011
00:55:03,064 --> 00:55:04,484
The crops were still growing.

1012
00:55:04,824 --> 00:55:06,744
The workers still had hands.

1013
00:55:07,244 --> 00:55:08,884
Nothing real was missing.

1014
00:55:09,424 --> 00:55:12,424
What was missing was numbers on a spreadsheet;

1015
00:55:12,524 --> 00:55:17,144
